      id: 'eng.cost.shouldcost',
      title: 'Should-cost and reading a supplier quote',
      pre: [
        {
          q: 'A labour-intensive product follows an 85% learning curve. Unit 1,000 took 20 minutes to make. How long does unit 2,000 take?',
          options: ['20 minutes', '10 minutes', '17 minutes', '3 minutes'],
          answer: '17 minutes',
          why: 'Each doubling of cumulative volume cuts the time per unit to 85%: 0.85 × 20 = 17 minutes.',
        },
      ],
      teach: [
        'A should-cost is your own estimate of what a part ought to cost at an efficient supplier, built bottom-up like a cost price: material by weight and price, process time per step × a market hour rate, setup, scrap, overhead and a reasonable profit margin.\n\nIt turns a price talk from haggling into facts: “Your quote implies 9 minutes of machining; we see 6. What are we missing?”',
        'Read a quote by its parts. Ask for a cost breakdown: material, processing, setup, tooling, packaging, logistics, margin.\n\nOne-off costs such as moulds and fixtures should be separate from the piece price, with clear ownership of the tool. Annual volume and batch size must be stated, because the piece price depends on both.',
        'Material often drives price changes. For metals and plastics, many contracts have a material index clause: the material part of the price follows a published price index, adjusted for example every quarter. Then the supplier needs no margin for speculation, and you carry the market risk openly.\n\nCheck which weight the index applies to: the finished weight, or the gross weight including offcuts.',
        'The learning curve: in labour-intensive work, every doubling of cumulative volume, all units made so far, cuts the time per unit to a fixed percentage, often 80 to 90%. With an 85% curve, unit 200 takes 85% of the time of unit 100.\n\nWright (1936) found it in aircraft building, using the average time over all units; the per-unit version used here is Crawford’s. Use it with new products: a price that stays flat over years of rising volume suggests the savings stay with the supplier.',
        'Value engineering means reviewing the drawing with the supplier to remove cost without losing function: a looser tolerance, a standard material, one operation fewer. Ask for their routing, the steps with machines and times.\n\nFailure modes: a blanket “minus 5%” for every supplier, or squeezing margin until the supplier cuts corners. Experts argue about open-book costing; many suppliers refuse to show their numbers. A sharp question: “Which line in your breakdown differs most from ours, and why?”',
      ],
      example: {
        kind: 'faded',
        text: 'A supplier quotes €17.50 per machined aluminium housing at 5,000 a year. Your should-cost: material 0.9 kg gross at €4.50 per kg = €4.05; machining 6 minutes at a market rate of €70 per hour = €7.00; setup €0.40 per part; scrap and overhead 15%; margin 8%. That gives (4.05 + 7.00 + 0.40) × 1.15 × 1.08 = about €14.20.\n\nPurchasing wants to demand €14.20.',
        ask: 'What would you do next?',
        reveal: 'Don’t just demand your number. Ask for their breakdown and compare line by line.\n\nIf they assume 9 minutes of machining, ask why: an older machine, extra operations, a different fixture? Maybe they see a tolerance or surface finish on the drawing that costs time you didn’t price, and relaxing it is cheaper for both.\n\nIf their material weight is higher, check the stock size they use. Agree on the facts first; the price follows.',
      },
      said: 'What engineers say: “Our should-cost says fourteen. Before we push, let’s see their routing. If they need nine minutes, maybe our drawing is the problem.”',
